==============================================================================
1. INTRODUCTION                                      *parley-introduction*

Parley lets you read and act on pull request review threads directly from
your buffer. Signs and virtual text mark commented lines, a floating window
shows the full discussion, and you can reply, edit, delete, and react
without leaving Neovim.

Parley is intentionally narrow: it focuses on line-level code review inside
the editing flow. For full GitHub clients (issues, PR metadata, repo
browsing), see octo.nvim or gh.nvim.

Current state: ~

  - Built-in providers: GitHub (git repos) and Arcanum (Arc/Arcadia repos)
  - Regular file buffers are supported
  - diffview.nvim integration is planned

==============================================================================
2. REQUIREMENTS                                      *parley-requirements*

  - Neovim >= 0.10
  - plenary.nvim (https://github.com/nvim-lua/plenary.nvim)

GitHub provider: ~
  - git
  - gh (GitHub CLI, https://cli.github.com)

Arcanum provider: ~
  - arc (Arcadia VCS CLI)
  - curl
  - HTTPS access to the configured API host (default arcanum.yandex.net)

Optional: ~

  - telescope.nvim — for discussion pickers (|parley-telescope|)
  - render-markdown.nvim — for rendered markdown in the discussion float

==============================================================================
3. SETUP                                             *parley-setup*

Call `require("parley").setup()` once from your Neovim config. All options
are optional; the defaults are shown in |parley-configuration|.

Minimal setup: >lua
    require("parley").setup({})
<

With lazy.nvim: >lua
    {
      "segoon/parley.nvim",
      dependencies = {
        "nvim-lua/plenary.nvim",
        -- Optional:
        -- "nvim-telescope/telescope.nvim",
        -- "MeanderingProgrammer/render-markdown.nvim",
      },
      opts = {},
    }
<

Quick start: ~

  1. Install the plugin and call `setup()`.
  2. Configure GitHub credentials with `gh auth login` or a supported token
     environment variable; for Arcanum, configure an OAuth token
     (|parley-provider-arcanum|).
  3. Open a regular file in a Git or Arc repo whose branch has an open PR.
     Arcanum requires a remote branch.
  4. Run `:Parley refresh` or re-enter the buffer.
  5. Navigate with `]c` / `[c` / `]C` / `[C` (|parley-keymaps|).
  6. Toggle the discussion window with `:Parley discussion toggle`.

If no matching PR is found, Parley stays silent and inactive.

==============================================================================
4. COMMANDS                                          *parley-commands*

All Parley commands support tab completion.

:Parley refresh                                      *:Parley-refresh*
    Force a re-fetch of PR discussions for the current buffer. Shows a
    progress popup while the request is in flight.

:Parley discussion resolve                          *:Parley-discussion-resolve*
    Resolve the selected open issue. With no selected thread, choose a discussion
    at the source cursor using the existing line picker.

:Parley discussion reopen                            *:Parley-discussion-reopen*
    Reopen the selected resolved issue, or choose one at the source cursor.
    Both commands work for general and unavailable-location threads opened with
    `:Parley discussion list`. They preserve the selected thread and composer text.
    Dropped, non-issue, unknown, incomplete, and cyclic threads cannot transition.
    No default keybindings are assigned. Unsupported providers explain why.

:Parley discussion list                              *:Parley-discussion-list*
    List every discussion in the active review using the built-in picker.
    General, whole-file, old-side, historical, and unavailable anchors open in
    a discussion float attached to the source buffer without jumping to a line.
    Replies and your own comment edits/deletions remain available in that float.

:Parley discussion open                              *:Parley-discussion-open*
    Open the discussion window for the current line.

:Parley discussion close                             *:Parley-discussion-close*
    Close the discussion window.

:Parley discussion toggle                            *:Parley-discussion-toggle*
    Toggle the discussion window for the current line.

:Parley discussion new                               *:Parley-discussion-new*
    Open a draft for a new top-level comment on the cursor line.

:'<,'>Parley discussion new                          *:Parley-discussion-new-range*
    Open a draft for a new comment spanning the visual selection.

:Parley discussion reply                             *:Parley-discussion-reply*
    Reply to the selected or current comment thread.

:Parley review actions                                *:Parley-review-actions*
    Open the provider's explicit review-action picker. Arcanum offers Ship,
    Sticky ship, Unship, Block merge, and Unblock merge. Confirm the PR, loaded
    revision, and your current verdict before submitting. Escape cancels.
    Sticky ship approves future diffs until withdrawn. No review message is sent.
    Withdrawals require your corresponding current verdict. No default keymap.

:Parley comment react                                *:Parley-comment-react*
    Open provider-specific choices for the selected comment. Add a reaction
    if absent, or remove one you already added. Arcanum preserves the chosen
    add/remove intent even if the local snapshot refreshes before submission.

:Parley comment edit                                 *:Parley-comment-edit*
    Edit the selected comment. Only works on your own comments.

:Parley comment delete                               *:Parley-comment-delete*
    Delete the selected comment with a confirmation prompt.
    Only works on your own comments.

:Parley nav buf-next                                 *:Parley-nav-buf-next*
    Jump to the next commented line in the current buffer.
    Wraps around at the end.

:Parley nav buf-prev                                 *:Parley-nav-buf-prev*
    Jump to the previous commented line in the current buffer.
    Wraps around at the start.

:Parley nav review-next                              *:Parley-nav-review-next*
    Jump to the next comment across all files in the review.
    Discussions are ordered by (file, line). When the next comment is in a
    different file, that file is opened with `:edit`. Wraps around at the end.

:Parley nav review-prev                              *:Parley-nav-review-prev*
    Jump to the previous comment across all files in the review.
    Mirror of `:Parley nav review-next`. Wraps around at the start.

:Parley quickfix                                     *:Parley-quickfix*
    Fill and open the quickfix list with file-associated discussions from the
    active review. Entries use review-wide mappings when available. Unavailable
    positions remain invalid rows; general discussions are omitted. Use
    `:Parley discussion list` to access every thread.

==============================================================================
5. KEYMAPS                                           *parley-keymaps*

Global keymaps (registered in setup): ~

    Key    Action
    `]c`     Jump to the next commented line in the buffer
    `[c`     Jump to the previous commented line in the buffer
    `]C`     Jump to the next comment in the whole review (cross-file)
    `[C`     Jump to the previous comment in the whole review (cross-file)

    All four are configurable via |parley-config-keymaps|. Set to `""` to
    disable.

Discussion window keymaps: ~

    Key          Action
    `q` / `<Esc>`  Close the discussion window
    `r`            Reply to the current thread
    `R`            React to the selected comment
    `e`            Edit the selected comment
    `d`            Delete the selected comment

Draft/input window keymaps: ~

    Key          Mode    Action
    `q` / `<Esc>`  n       Close draft (prompts if non-empty)
    `s`            n       Submit the draft
    `<C-s>`        i       Submit the draft
    `C`            n       Cancel the in-flight request

Reaction and review-action picker keymaps: ~

    Key        Action
    `q` / `<Esc>`  Cancel
    `<CR>`       Confirm selection
    `j` / `<Down>` Next item
    `k` / `<Up>`   Previous item

==============================================================================
6. TELESCOPE                                         *parley-telescope*

When `telescope = true` (the default) and telescope.nvim is installed,
Parley registers two extensions on setup:

  `parley_discussions`       All discussions in the current PR.
  `parley_discussions_file`  Discussions for the current file only.

Usage: >lua
    require("telescope").extensions.parley_discussions.parley_discussions()
    require("telescope").extensions.parley_discussions_file.parley_discussions_file()
<

Selecting a located entry opens the file, refreshes Parley state, navigates to
the discussion, and opens its window. General, historical, old-side, and other
unavailable-location entries open against the source buffer without a fabricated
line jump. The review-wide picker includes these threads.

If you do not use Telescope, disable the extension loading: >lua
    require("parley").setup({ telescope = false })
<

==============================================================================
7. STATUSLINE                                        *parley-statusline*

Parley exposes a statusline component via |parley.statusline(bufnr)|.
Provider branding comes from public provider metadata: GitHub displays
`GitHub PR #…`, Arcanum displays `Arcanum PR #…`, and custom providers supply their
own name. Review state without a provider snapshot displays `PR #…`.

When the current buffer has an active PR, it returns a string like: >
    GitHub PR #42 . approved . 3 unresolved
<

When inactive, it returns an empty string, so it can be safely included
unconditionally.

With lualine: >lua
    {
      "nvim-lualine/lualine.nvim",
      opts = function(_, opts)
        table.insert(opts.sections.lualine_c, function()
          return require("parley").statusline()
        end)
      end,
    }
<

With the built-in statusline: >vim
    set statusline+=%{%v:lua.require('parley').statusline()%}
<

==============================================================================
8. CONFIGURATION                                     *parley-configuration*

<parley-configuration-start>
<parley-configuration-end>

Provider defaults are merged separately; see |parley-providers|.

`refresh_interval` is accepted but currently has no effect. Periodic refresh is
not implemented. Buffer entry may reuse cached data; `:Parley refresh` forces a
fetch. Writes refresh remote state, and local edits update mappings separately.

==============================================================================
9. HIGHLIGHTS                                        *parley-highlights*

All highlight groups are defined with `default = true`, so your colorscheme
or manual `vim.api.nvim_set_hl()` calls take precedence.

ParleySign                                           *hl-ParleySign*
    Gutter sign for commented lines.
    Default link: `DiagnosticSignInfo`

ParleyStaleSign                                      *hl-ParleyStaleSign*
    Gutter sign for lines whose anchor has diverged (stale).
    Default link: `Comment`

ParleyVirtualTextMeta                                *hl-ParleyVirtualTextMeta*
    Author and timestamp text below commented lines.
    Default link: `DiagnosticVirtualTextHint`

ParleyStaleVirtualTextMeta                           *hl-ParleyStaleVirtualTextMeta*
    Author and timestamp text for stale comments.
    Default link: `Comment`

ParleyVirtualText                                    *hl-ParleyVirtualText*
    Comment body text below commented lines.
    Default link: `DiagnosticVirtualTextInfo`

ParleyStaleVirtualText                               *hl-ParleyStaleVirtualText*
    Comment body text for stale comments.
    Default link: `Comment`

Example override: >lua
    vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, "ParleySign", { fg = "#e0af68" })
<

==============================================================================
10. PROVIDERS                                        *parley-providers*

Parley uses a provider abstraction to support multiple hosting platforms.

Local revision reads and new-comment validation use the detected VCS (Git or
Arc). Mapping compares review-head content with a loaded buffer's text, including
unsaved edits, or with the working-tree file when no buffer is loaded. Edits
refresh positions after a short debounce without an API request. Separate
checkouts have independent mappings even when they share the same review.

If content cannot be read, existing locations remain visible as stale
approximations with a warning. Deleted lines have no precise local position;
quickfix entries are marked unavailable rather than targeting the original line.

New comments require local HEAD to match the review revision, a clean file, and
no unsaved changes. Every selected line must belong to the review's changed
new-side lines. Validation runs before composition and again before sending.
Failures preserve the draft; Parley never bypasses a failed VCS check.

Internal VCS helpers take a VcsInfo table rather than a root string. Shared
workflows use DetectedReview.head_sha; write_context remains provider-private.

GitHub                                               *parley-provider-github*

The built-in GitHub provider uses the `gh` CLI for API calls and supports
both github.com and GitHub Enterprise Server (GHES) instances.

Authentication: ~

Tokens are resolved in the following order:

  1. Environment variables:
     - `GH_TOKEN` / `GITHUB_TOKEN` (for github.com and *.ghe.com)
     - `GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN` / `GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN` (for GHES)
  2. The `oauth_token` field in `~/.config/gh/hosts.yml`

Run `gh auth login` to set up authentication.

Provider options: ~

GitHub-specific options live under `providers.github` in the setup call. Defaults: >lua
    require("parley").setup({
      providers = {
        github = {
          timeout_ms = 5000,
          retry_count = 2,
          retry_base_delay_ms = 250,
          retry_max_delay_ms = 2000,
        },
      },
    })
<

Limitations: ~

  - `resolve` / `unresolve` are not supported (require GraphQL).
  - `submit_review` is supported: "approve", "request_changes", "comment".

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Arcanum                                              *parley-provider-arcanum*

The built-in Arcanum provider supports Arc (Arcadia VCS) repositories via
the Arcanum public and plugin REST APIs over HTTPS at arcanum.yandex.net.

The provider is activated automatically when the current buffer belongs to an
Arc repository (detected via `arc root`).

Authentication: ~

OAuth tokens are resolved in the following order:

  1. Environment variable: `ARCANUM_TOKEN`
  2. Environment variable: `ARC_OAUTH_TOKEN`
  3. File named by `ARC_TOKEN_PATH`
  4. File: `~/.arc/token` (plain text, single line)

Values are trimmed; empty environment values are ignored. An explicitly selected
ARC_TOKEN_PATH must be readable and nonempty. Failure does not silently switch to
~/.arc/token. Diagnostics identify the source without printing the token.

To obtain a token: https://a.yandex-team.ru/oauth/token

User login detection: ~

The local Arc login comes from `arc info --json` and is diagnostic metadata only.
Normal review loading verifies the OAuth account via `/v2/users/me?fields=name`
before restoring cached discussions. Only this verified login determines comment
ownership. A differing local Arc login does not override it. Failed verification
fails review loading; Parley does not fall back to a guessed account or cached
ownership. Credential changes invalidate the session and reject obsolete results.
Direct provider mutations require a prepared session from review loading.

PR detection follows prefix-search pages using offset and has_next until an exact
remote branch match is found. Candidate details are fetched once per search;
malformed or nonprogressing pages are errors. Without a remote branch, Parley stays
inactive without authentication or PR HTTP requests.

Inline comments: ~

New line and range comments are published on the new side of the loaded review
using the V2 diff API. Submission uses the loaded diff without checking whether
Arcanum has since activated a newer diff. Refresh the review to use newer data.

Missing diff/revision metadata, unavailable file entries, and failed lookups
prevent submission; inline comments never fall back to general PR comments.
The draft remains available after failure or cancellation. If a creation response
is incomplete, check the review before retrying: the comment may have been sent.
Cancellation after sending does not guarantee that the server discarded it.

Discussion locations and issue states: ~

Arcanum threads retain nested replies, including incomplete or cyclic ancestry.
Only open issues contribute to the unresolved count; resolved, dropped, non-issue,
and unknown states remain distinguishable. Only verified current new-side inline
anchors receive buffer positions. Other anchors retain their side, paths, and
revision metadata in the discussion list and float. Navigation skips unavailable
positions; quickfix retains file-associated entries as invalid rows and omits
general discussions. Discussion selection and composer text survive refreshes.

Provider options: ~

The configured host must be a hostname or bracketed IPv6 address, optionally with
a port. Schemes, userinfo, paths, queries, fragments, and whitespace are rejected.
An explicit constructor host overrides providers.arcanum.host; the effective host
is used consistently by verification, discovery, reads, writes, and cache identity.

Arcanum-specific options live under `providers.arcanum` in the setup call: >lua
    require("parley").setup({
      providers = {
        arcanum = {
          timeout_ms = 10000,      -- Queue + attempts + retry waits, milliseconds
          retry_count = 2,         -- Maximum automatic retries for eligible requests
          retry_base_delay_ms = 250,
          retry_max_delay_ms = 2000,
          request_interval_ms = 1000, -- Minimum spacing between request starts
          idempotent_write_retries = false, -- Requires confirmed server support
          host = "arcanum.yandex.net",  -- API hostname (rarely changed)
        },
      },
    })
<

Each transport request has one `timeout_ms` budget covering queueing, HTTP
attempts, and retry waits. Inline entry lookup and comment creation are separate
requests. Expiration completes the request and terminates any active curl process.

Requests sharing a host and OAuth token share a queue in this Neovim process.
The default spacing is one second; retries use the same queue. Separate Neovim
processes and other clients are not coordinated. A shared queue retains the
slowest interval used during this process. A 429 response delays that queue;
`Retry-After` seconds or a GMT HTTP-date takes precedence over shorter backoff.
Long server-directed waits remain subject to the request's deadline.

Reads, including PR search, retry transient failures up to `retry_count` times.
Comment creation and replies always send one idempotency key per submission,
reused across automatic retries with the same body. Their automatic retries are
disabled unless `idempotent_write_retries = true`. Enable this only after confirming
that your deployed Arcanum server supports `Idempotency-Key`; local server source
alone does not establish deployment support. Edits, deletions, and unclassified
mutations are not retried automatically. Conflicts and authentication failures
are not retried.

Manual resubmission uses a new key. After an uncertain write failure or
cancellation, check the review before retrying. Drafts are preserved; terminating
curl cannot roll back a change the server has already accepted.

Limitations: ~

  - Reactions require GENERIC_WRITE. Ship/sticky ship/block merge require
    REVIEW_REQUEST_SHIP; withdrawing approval or a block requires GENERIC_WRITE.
    Ordinary tokens cannot query the admin-only authorities endpoint. Permissions
    are enforced by the server; capability support does not verify token scopes.
  - :Parley review actions rechecks the active diff after confirmation and aborts
    on mismatch. The server cannot atomically pin the expected diff, so a diff
    activated between the recheck and mutation can still receive the action.
    Mutations are never automatically retried. Check uncertain outcomes before
    retrying. Generic submit_review with a body remains unsupported.
  - Review status uses plugin review data: any block means changes_requested;
    zero remaining required approvals means approved; otherwise pending. A failed
    or malformed status response means unknown and disables review actions while
    discussions remain readable. Refresh to recover.
  - Issue resolution/reopening uses the public comment PATCH API and requires
    GENERIC_WRITE authorization. Capability support does not guarantee permission.
    These PATCH requests are never retried automatically. After uncertain failures
    or cancellation, Parley refreshes the review; check it before retrying.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Provider action capabilities: ~                       *parley-provider-capabilities*

Optional `provider:capabilities(review)` returns a table keyed by provider method:
`post_top_level_comment`, `reply`, `edit`, `delete`, `react`, `resolve`, `unresolve`,
`submit_review`, and `review_action`. Each value is `{ available = true }` or
`{ available = false, reason = "Explanation" }`. This hook must be local-only and
must not yield. It describes implemented actions, not token permissions.
When the hook exists, missing or malformed action entries fail closed.
Providers without it retain existing actions; resolve/reopen and review_action require explicit
support. Reaction choices remain required for reaction mutation.

Built-in action support (yes = implemented in Parley): ~

  Action                    GitHub  Arcanum
  post_top_level_comment    yes     yes
  reply                     yes     yes
  edit                      yes     yes
  delete                    yes     yes
  react                     yes     yes
  resolve                   no      yes
  unresolve                 no      yes
  submit_review             yes     no
  review_action             no      yes

Capability checks run before composing, choosing reactions, confirming deletion,
and submitting writes. Provider reasons explain unavailable actions. Commands
remain discoverable in completion. Custom providers may implement cancellable
`begin_resolve(review, discussion_id, callback)` and `begin_unresolve(...)` with
the existing WriteResult/CancelHandle contract; coroutine methods remain supported.

Provider configuration lifecycle: ~

Built-in defaults and registration are owned by `parley.providers`. Each `setup()`
binds a copy of the merged provider settings to its registered factories. Existing
instances keep their settings when options or `require("parley").config` change.
Call `setup()` again to apply changed settings to subsequently created instances.

Direct built-in constructors accept `config = { ... }` with provider-specific
options and copy it over that provider's defaults. Omitting `config` uses those
defaults. Arcanum's explicit constructor `host` overrides `config.host`; both
request URLs and cache identity use the selected host. `retry_count = 0` disables
retries.

Custom providers: ~

After calling `setup()`, you can register additional providers: >lua
    local registry = require("parley.registry")
    registry.register({
      name = "MyProvider",
      detect = function(vcs_info) ... end,
      factory = function(opts) ... end,
      health = function(context)
        -- Optional: local-only diagnostics; never construct a provider here.
        return { { level = "info", message = "Local diagnostics complete" } }
      end,
    })
<

The provider must implement all required methods in `parley.Provider` and expose
`display_name`, a nonblank string such as `"MyProvider"`. This metadata is required
on every instance returned by the factory; the registration spec's `name` does
not substitute for it. Existing custom providers must add this field to satisfy
provider validation. The statusline uses this name without inferring a provider
from review URLs or private cache fields.
See `lua/parley/provider.lua` for the full interface.

Every provider must implement `validate_comment_target(review, target)`. The
`target` contains `vcs_info`, `rel_path` (repository-relative), and `anchor`
(`start_line`, optional `end_line`). Return `{ ok = true }` to accept or
`{ ok = false, err = "Reason shown to the user" }` to reject; the reason must be
nonblank. This method runs in a Plenary async context and must not post comments.
It runs before opening the composer and again before submission. Errors and
malformed results block the operation; submission failures preserve the draft.

GitHub and Arcanum require every selected line to belong to the review diff.
Custom providers choose their own eligibility policy, but unsaved-buffer,
checkout-state, and context-race checks remain mandatory shared protections.
Existing custom providers must add this method; there is no implicit fallback.

Internal integrations using `parley.vcs.check_anchor_in_diff` should migrate to
the provider method. Generic diff reads use `parley.vcs.read_diff(vcs_info,
base_branch, rel_path, head_sha)`, returning diff text or `nil, error`.
The `head_sha` argument is required and forwarded unchanged to the VCS adapter;
there is no implicit current-revision default. Callers that omitted it must pass
the detected review's `head_sha`. Missing, empty, non-string, or leading-hyphen
revisions return `nil, "review revision is unavailable"` before command execution.


Optional cancellable writes: ~

Providers may implement either or both of these colon-call methods:

  - `begin_post_top_level_comment(review, file, anchor, body, callback)`
  - `begin_reply(review, discussion, parent_comment, body, callback)`

When absent, shared writes call the corresponding required coroutine method.
When present, these methods must be functions. The same optional-method validation
applies to `reaction_choices` and `reaction_presentation`; omit unavailable hooks
with `nil`, not `false` or another placeholder value.

Starters must return promptly without yielding, returning a `parley.CancelHandle`
with a `cancel()` function (called without a self argument). Invoke the
`parley.WriteCallback` exactly once on the Neovim main loop, either before returning
or later. A `parley.WriteResult` contains `ok` (boolean), optional `comment`,
optional `err` (string), and optional boolean `cancelled`, `uncertain`, and
`refresh`. Set `refresh = true` for a known conflict requiring fresh remote state.
Set `uncertain = true` when a failed write may have reached the server,
with an `err` explaining that the user should check the review before retrying.
This message remains visible in the composer after cancellation or failure.
A cancelled result must have `ok = false`. Successful callbacks need not return
a comment object.

Cancellation requests completion through the callback; shared writes continue
waiting until it arrives. It does not guarantee rollback of a remote mutation.
Shared execution tolerates immediate completion, ignores duplicate or stale
callbacks, and turns startup failures or malformed pending handles/results into
write failures while retaining drafts. Existing providers with valid hooks need
no behavioral changes. Providers without these hooks retain coroutine fallbacks.

Shared HTTP infrastructure: ~

`require("parley.http").start(opts, callback)` returns a cancel handle immediately.
Options are `url`, optional `method`, `headers`, serialized `body`, Bearer `token`,
and `timeout_ms` (default 10000). Its callback completes once on the main loop.
A result has `ok`, `sent`, and either `response` or `err`; failures may also carry
`exit`, `cancelled`, or `timed_out`. Network success is distinct from HTTP success:
`result.response.ok` indicates a 2xx status. Response fields are `status`,
`headers`, and `body`. `http.request(opts)` awaits the same operation through
Plenary and returns the response, raising on network failure. `http.get` and
`http.post` retain their convenience interfaces. Provider-specific scheduling,
retries, and response-envelope validation belong to the provider layer.

Provider-owned cache identity: ~

Every provider must implement `provider:cache_identity()`. It runs in a Plenary
coroutine and returns { provider, host, repository, account }, with four nonempty
strings, or nil when stable local credential identity is unavailable. `account`
must be a non-secret fingerprint; never return credentials or include them in
errors. Identity derivation belongs under `lua/parley/providers/` and must not
make network requests.

Parley validates provider construction and identity before publishing provider
snapshots. Missing methods and malformed values are contract errors. Nil identity
keeps live review operations available using isolated temporary state and skips
all persistent review-cache reads, writes, and invalidation.

Review cache keys use the reviews-v3 namespace and hashes of ordered identity
fields and typed branch/PR identifiers. Provider, host, repository, and credential
contexts are isolated. Token rotation causes a cold refresh. Checkout-specific
local mappings remain separate even when remote cache identity is shared.

Legacy review-cache entries are left untouched and never used as a fallback.
The first refresh after this change fetches fresh data. Identity changes during
fetching discard those results and schedule a refresh with the current identity.

`registry.resolve_with_opts(vcs_info)` returns the validated provider and its
opaque detection options. `registry.resolve(vcs_info)` retains its provider-only
return value. Shared cache code consumes validated snapshot identity, not factory
options or private provider fields.

The optional `provider:prepare(vcs_info)` hook runs in a Plenary coroutine before
provider identity is published or review caches are consulted. Preparation errors
clear obsolete provider/review state. Providers without the hook retain their
existing lifecycle. Arcanum verifies its API account here; its cache identity binds
host, credential fingerprint, and verified viewer and excludes the local Arc login.
The ownership cache version changes with this release, so earlier cached ownership
is not reused. Tokens are neither stored in cache keys nor printed in diagnostics.

The optional provider-spec `health(context)` callback runs in a plenary.async
coroutine. It receives `vcs_info`, the `opts` returned by detection, and current
`config`. Return a list of `{ level, message }` entries, where level is `ok`,
`info`, `warn`, or `error`. Keep diagnostics local: no API requests, provider
construction, or credentials in messages. A missing callback is informational.
Concrete diagnostic implementations belong under `lua/parley/providers/`.


Provider-owned reactions: ~

Providers may implement these optional, synchronous, local-only methods:

  reaction_choices(review, comment)
      Returns an ordered list of { reaction, label, emoji?, remove_only? } and an optional
      unavailable-reason string. `reaction` is an opaque provider identifier.
      An empty list disables mutation and displays the reason when requested.

  reaction_presentation(reaction_type)
      Returns { label, emoji? } for an existing reaction identifier. Shared UI
      adds counts and viewer state. Unknown identifiers should retain their
      original value as the label.

These methods use the usual provider:method(...) calling convention. Define
concrete catalogs and presentation logic under `lua/parley/providers/`.
Missing methods preserve raw labels and make interactive mutations unavailable;
there is no implicit GitHub catalog. Cached reaction data remains unchanged.

GitHub retains its existing reaction choices and emoji. Arcanum offers thumbs up,
thumbs down, and heart (:+1:, :-1:, :heart:). Other codes remain readable and can
be removed if you added them. AI comments allow only one reaction per account;
a conflict requires refreshing and explicitly removing your existing reaction.
The public comment response does not identify AI comments reliably.
The picker and direct reaction service reject choices that are not currently
offered. Changing review/provider context while the picker is open requires
reopening it before submitting.

Optional cancellable action hooks: ~

  begin_set_reaction(review, comment_id, code, present, callback)
      Sets the captured desired state; present=true adds, false removes. Shared
      UI revalidates comment availability. Without this hook, react() toggles.
      remove_only choices cannot add reactions.

  review_actions(review)
      Local-only ordered { action, label, confirmation, reason? } choices.
      A reason disables that choice. Requires explicit review_action capability.

  begin_review_action(review, action, callback)
      Executes a confirmed action. The shared UI verifies review identity after
      selection and confirmation; providers validate their own remote context.

Both begin hooks follow the WriteCallback/CancelHandle contract above, including
exactly-once completion, cancellation and uncertain outcomes. A failed result may
set refresh=true for known conflicts that require a fresh snapshot. Refresh preserves
selected discussions and drafts. GitHub's submit_review contract is unchanged.

Custom VCS implementations: ~

After `setup()`, register both a detector and an adapter via `parley.vcs`.
Concrete VCS modules belong under `lua/parley/providers/`; a module can be
reused by multiple hosting providers. For example: >lua
    local vcs = require("parley.vcs")
    local custom = require("parley.providers.my_vcs")
    vcs.register_detector("my-vcs", custom.detect)
    vcs.register_adapter("my-vcs", custom.adapter)
<
The detector runs in a plenary.async coroutine and returns a `parley.VcsInfo`
with `vcs = "my-vcs"`, or nil when the path is not supported.

The adapter must provide these functions (called without a `self` argument):

  head()                  Command argument array to read the current revision
  show(revision, path)    Command argument array to read immutable file contents
  status(path)            Command argument array to inspect file status
  dirty(output)           Parse status stdout: true if dirty, false if clean,
                          or nil plus an error string if parsing fails
  diff(base, head, path)  Command argument array producing a zero-context
                          unified review diff, with the review head on new side

Commands run asynchronously in the repository root. Adapter registration rejects
empty names, duplicate names, and missing/non-function methods. Adapter names
must match `VcsInfo.vcs`; unknown names fail without executing commands.

`vcs.reset_adapters()` removes adapters without changing detectors.
`vcs.reset_detectors()` removes detectors without changing adapters.
Every `setup()` resets both and registers built-ins, checking Arc before Git.
Re-register custom implementations after each setup. Direct revision/validation
calls before setup require explicit adapter registration.

==============================================================================
11. HEALTH CHECK                                     *parley-health*

Run: >vim
    :checkhealth parley
<

The health check verifies:

  - Neovim version (>= 0.10) and `plenary.async` availability
  - Configuration state and cache-directory accessibility
  - Optional integrations (telescope.nvim, render-markdown.nvim)
  - The source file's repository using registered VCS detectors
  - Local diagnostics supplied by the matching provider

GitHub diagnostics check Git/gh executables, branch, and credentials from the
environment or hosts.yml. Keyring credentials are not checked. Arcanum diagnostics
check Arc/curl executables, remote branch, configured HTTPS host, local Arc login,
and credential source/availability. They do not construct or prepare a provider.
Missing executables for unrelated providers do not produce warnings.

Credential availability does not verify server authentication. No API requests or
PR detection are performed. Tokens are never included in built-in diagnostics.

When :checkhealth opens its report buffer, Parley uses the regular alternate
buffer as the source file. If unavailable, repository diagnostics are skipped.
Repository checks also require setup() and Plenary; checks time out after ten
seconds, and late results are discarded. Providers without diagnostic callbacks
and repositories without matching providers are reported informationally.

==============================================================================
12. LUA API                                          *parley-api*

<parley-api-start>
<parley-api-end>

